WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
Defensive Rust programming is a layered
engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.415
Start by using Rust's type system to model
valid states.

3
00:00:06.495 --> 00:00:10.308
Prefer domain types over ambiguous
primitives when the distinction matters.

4
00:00:10.388 --> 00:00:12.179
Use enums to model states and intent.

5
00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:15.090
Make absence and failure explicit with
Option and Result.

6
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:19.157
Validate input at trust boundaries and
convert it into types that downstream code

7
00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:19.757
can trust.

8
00:00:19.837 --> 00:00:21.281
Handle errors deliberately.

9
00:00:21.361 --> 00:00:25.001
Review unwrap, expect, panic paths, retry
behavior, rejection behavior,

10
00:00:25.081 --> 00:00:26.467
and fail-closed decisions.

11
00:00:26.547 --> 00:00:30.592
Keep error messages useful without
exposing secrets or sensitive implementation

12
00:00:30.672 --> 00:00:31.076
detail.

13
00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:33.871
Treat unsafe code and FFI as small,
documented, tested,

14
00:00:33.951 --> 00:00:35.511
and reviewed trust boundaries.

15
00:00:35.591 --> 00:00:39.635
Govern dependencies, features, build
inputs, secrets, logs, and configuration.

16
00:00:39.715 --> 00:00:41.911
Review crates and transitive dependencies.

17
00:00:41.991 --> 00:00:44.186
Understand which feature flags are
enabled.

18
00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:48.368
Protect secrets from source code, logs,
artifacts, and accidental serialization.

19
00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:52.377
Validate configuration and use structured
logging with deliberate redaction.

20
00:00:52.457 --> 00:00:56.097
Design concurrency and async behavior with
ownership, synchronization,

21
00:00:56.177 --> 00:00:59.585
backpressure, cancellation, timeouts, and
resource limits in mind.

22
00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:03.363
Test expected behavior, boundary cases,
negative cases, and regressions.

23
00:01:03.443 --> 00:01:05.349
Use tooling, code review, monitoring,

24
00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:08.029
and incident lessons to keep improving the
codebase.

25
00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:11.749
The main goal is Rust software that is
explicit, constrained, reviewable,

26
00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:13.620
tested, observable, and resilient.
